  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The rule is enabled and its last run status is `ok`.\n\n**1. Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, ID `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`). Its time field is `timestamp`. The fields that matter here are `timestamp` (date), `response` (text, with `response.keyword` for exact matching), `clientip`, `bytes`, `url`, and `request`. The full list has about 45 fields.\n\n**2. Dashboard:** 'S4-fafc53a0 Web logs' (ID `d1820716-f533-4389-9c5e-84ba20958114`) with exactly two panels, both on that data view:\n- 'Requests over time': a line chart of count by date histogram on `timestamp`.\n- 'Requests by response code': a donut of count by `response.keyword`, with up to 10 terms.\n\n**3. Alert rule:** 'S4-fafc53a0 5xx errors' (ID `9331b2d2-56da-4369-82d7-efaf45cd2932`), an `.es-query` rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- Runs every 1m. Fires when the count is greater than 10 over a 5m window.\n- The query matches `response.keyword` against the regex `5[0-9][0-9]`, so it covers all 500\u2013599 codes as strings.\n- The rule was created enabled.\n- The first version used `{{date_start}}`/`{{date}}` placeholders in the time range. Elasticsearch rejected them and every run failed. I changed the range to `now-5m` to `now` and updated the rule in place. The rule now runs with status `ok`.\n\n**4. Panel rename:** the response-code panel is now 'Responses by code'. I read the dashboard back and it has the two panels, 'Requests over time' and 'Responses by code'.\n\nI did not run the aggregations directly. The data-query endpoints are disabled on this Kibana, so I couldn't check the distinct response-code values. The regex is the match I'd expect for 500\u2013599, but I haven't seen the rule count any real 5xx documents.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
